00:22:41.920 --> 00:22:46.900
Lauren Hallman wrote in to say, at 1 minute 47 seconds, there's a weird circular object

504
00:22:46.900 --> 00:22:51.160
in the bottom right next to an ancient stapler that I can't identify.

505
00:22:51.680 --> 00:22:56.360
And Elizabeth Molina said, what's the deal with the scale behind Jenna?

506
00:22:56.360 --> 00:23:00.320
It looks like a food scale, which would make more sense in the kitchen or break room.

507
00:23:00.480 --> 00:23:02.720
I think they're talking about the same object.

508
00:23:03.080 --> 00:23:04.440
I froze on it.

509
00:23:04.660 --> 00:23:05.180
Yeah.

510
00:23:05.460 --> 00:23:06.720
It is a scale.

511
00:23:07.320 --> 00:23:12.340
I think it's a scale that Pam is using to weigh small packages for mailing.

512
00:23:12.820 --> 00:23:16.660
Yeah, I would think it was probably to make sure she has the right postage.

513
00:23:17.140 --> 00:23:18.580
That's what it looked like to me.

514
00:23:19.000 --> 00:23:19.420
Yeah.

515
00:23:19.420 --> 00:23:22.740
We don't have a mail room in our office.

516
00:23:22.880 --> 00:23:24.280
We have a mail cart.

517
00:23:25.000 --> 00:23:30.300
So I guess Pam has to keep that scale behind her, but it's not always there.

518
00:23:31.260 --> 00:23:34.500
Listen, we don't know what Pam does back there.

519
00:23:36.520 --> 00:23:40.280
With her revolving plants and her scale.

520
00:23:40.800 --> 00:23:42.720
She can't keep a plant alive.

521
00:23:42.720 --> 00:23:44.480
She has a giant shredder.

522
00:23:44.880 --> 00:23:45.440
Yeah.

523
00:23:45.660 --> 00:23:46.840
And now she has a scale.

524
00:23:47.220 --> 00:23:48.320
We don't know.

525
00:23:48.460 --> 00:23:49.300
We don't know.

526
00:23:50.420 --> 00:23:59.400
Well, guys, now we are going to find out that Dwight is taking Ryan on this sales call,

527
00:23:59.580 --> 00:24:01.040
his first sales call.

528
00:24:01.280 --> 00:24:02.640
Oh, he's so excited.

529
00:24:02.900 --> 00:24:07.560
There are some great deleted scenes on the DVD that are all about this storyline.

530
00:24:08.200 --> 00:24:09.760
But Dwight is so excited.

531
00:24:09.940 --> 00:24:14.440
He's hoping that Ryan will become part of the Dwight's army of champions, right?

532
00:24:14.760 --> 00:24:15.140
Yeah.

533
00:24:15.440 --> 00:24:16.900
Not be a slacker like Jim.

534
00:24:16.900 --> 00:24:20.380
This is like, and maybe, maybe become one of his best friends.

535
00:24:20.940 --> 00:24:21.300
Maybe.

536
00:24:21.540 --> 00:24:24.840
So in the DVDs, there's a deleted scene that I absolutely loved.

537
00:24:25.000 --> 00:24:26.420
I laughed so hard.

538
00:24:26.580 --> 00:24:28.580
And it's just such a simple moment, you guys.

539
00:24:28.580 --> 00:24:35.020
But Michael goes to hug Ryan to wish him well as he goes out for his first sale.

540
00:24:35.320 --> 00:24:37.460
You know, like, I'm going to give you a man hug.

541
00:24:37.640 --> 00:24:38.360
It's awkward.

542
00:24:38.620 --> 00:24:39.680
It's so awkward.

543
00:24:40.220 --> 00:24:45.100
But then it gets even weirder because then Dwight joins in and he wants to hug him too.

544
00:24:45.320 --> 00:24:48.380
And then Michael's like shoving Dwight, like, Dwight, get away.

545
00:24:48.660 --> 00:24:51.980
Like, meanwhile, like, BJ is like stuck between them.

546
00:24:52.120 --> 00:24:54.340
It is, it's, it just made me laugh so hard.

547
00:24:54.340 --> 00:24:56.300
I love this whole storyline.

548
00:24:56.860 --> 00:24:58.240
We know you love it too.

549
00:24:58.280 --> 00:24:59.500
We have a surprise.

550
00:24:59.860 --> 00:25:01.040
We have a surprise.

551
00:25:01.220 --> 00:25:01.660
Tell them, Jenna.

552
00:25:02.380 --> 00:25:06.680
Rain and BJ are going to come on the show today to talk all about it.

553
00:25:06.760 --> 00:25:08.580
So we're going to break down the rest of the episode.

554
00:25:08.760 --> 00:25:14.420
And then when they hop on, they will discuss all things Shroot Farms and the initiation.

555
00:25:14.860 --> 00:25:16.700
Yes, we're going to skip their scenes for now.

556
00:25:17.560 --> 00:25:19.700
But don't worry, we will get to it.

557
00:25:19.820 --> 00:25:23.120
For now, let's discuss what's going on at Stanford.

558
00:25:23.120 --> 00:25:24.280
Oh, yeah.

559
00:25:24.540 --> 00:25:29.640
At three minutes, 21 seconds, Karen notices that Jim took her chair and has replaced it

560
00:25:29.640 --> 00:25:31.220
with the squeaky chair.

561
00:25:31.440 --> 00:25:32.720
I want you to know something.

562
00:25:32.920 --> 00:25:35.620
Squeaky chair was not in the original script.

563
00:25:36.220 --> 00:25:36.420
Huh.

564
00:25:36.680 --> 00:25:41.280
The original storyline for Stanford was completely different.

565
00:25:41.440 --> 00:25:42.460
This was a late addition.

566
00:25:42.640 --> 00:25:47.780
And I traded some text with BJ and he said he doesn't quite remember where the squeaky chair

567
00:25:47.780 --> 00:25:48.720
storyline came from.

568
00:25:49.560 --> 00:25:55.320
Let me give you a little pitch where it might have come from and it might not have, but I

569
00:25:55.320 --> 00:26:01.940
want you guys to know something happening behind the scenes was over in the accounting

570
00:26:01.940 --> 00:26:02.380
department.

571
00:26:02.700 --> 00:26:04.040
And I texted Oscar.

572
00:26:04.240 --> 00:26:05.580
I was like, Oscar, do you remember this?

573
00:26:05.660 --> 00:26:07.100
And he just wrote back, ha, ha, ha, ha, ha.

574
00:26:07.440 --> 00:26:07.740
OK.

575
00:26:08.680 --> 00:26:11.520
There was a crap chair over in accounting.

576
00:26:11.760 --> 00:26:15.900
It was an absolute piece of crap chair that it wouldn't go up and down.

577
00:26:16.060 --> 00:26:17.760
It just was like set on one setting.

578
00:26:17.760 --> 00:26:19.840
So it was always way too low for my desk.

579
00:26:20.000 --> 00:26:22.380
And then it leaned way too far back.

580
00:26:22.560 --> 00:26:26.660
So you always felt like like, I mean, you guys, I know I'm Southern, but I always felt

581
00:26:26.660 --> 00:26:28.020
like I was going to get bucked out of it.

582
00:26:28.140 --> 00:26:30.020
I'd be like, that's the chair that's going to buck me out.

583
00:26:31.500 --> 00:26:35.960
So sometimes I would go and it would be at my desk and I'd be like, son of a gun, Oscar

584
00:26:35.960 --> 00:26:36.940
switched it again.

585
00:26:37.180 --> 00:26:39.280
It just traded back and forth between me and Oscar.

586
00:26:39.480 --> 00:26:43.100
I would move his chair and I'd put the crap chair at his desk.

587
00:26:43.240 --> 00:26:47.460
And then out of nowhere after lunch, it would be back at my desk.

588
00:26:47.560 --> 00:26:51.180
And I'd be like, Oscar, you gave me this piece of crap chair again.

589
00:26:51.940 --> 00:26:54.560
We must have done this, I think, for weeks.

590
00:26:54.560 --> 00:26:57.960
And then one day I moved it over to Creed's desk.

591
00:26:57.960 --> 00:26:59.780
Oh, no, Angela.

592
00:27:00.400 --> 00:27:01.120
I did.

593
00:27:01.360 --> 00:27:05.740
And I said to Oscar, I was like, I put it, I put that crap chair at Creed's desk.

594
00:27:05.900 --> 00:27:06.940
And we like laughed.

595
00:27:07.220 --> 00:27:11.420
And anyway, it just cracks me up that this was actually something happening.

596
00:27:11.640 --> 00:27:13.760
I don't know if the writers ever even knew about it.

597
00:27:13.840 --> 00:27:15.360
I don't know if we vented about it.

598
00:27:15.480 --> 00:27:18.320
But this was an actual thing happening over in accounting.

599
00:27:18.820 --> 00:27:21.540
Oh, my gosh, you had your own chair war.

600
00:27:21.840 --> 00:27:22.660
We did.

601
00:27:22.700 --> 00:27:25.360
But I don't know how Brian stayed out of it.

602
00:27:25.480 --> 00:27:27.820
Maybe because he was too far in the corner.

603
00:27:28.300 --> 00:27:28.740
Yeah.

604
00:27:29.040 --> 00:27:30.580
It just traded between me and Oscar.

605
00:27:31.260 --> 00:27:37.680
Well, did you notice in this scene in Stanford when Jim wheels his chair over to the copier?

606
00:27:37.860 --> 00:27:39.040
Did you look in the background?

607
00:27:40.260 --> 00:27:42.660
I noticed a little something back there.

608
00:27:42.900 --> 00:27:44.880
I saw a few people write in.

609
00:27:44.980 --> 00:27:46.840
You have to tell them because I think this is so beautiful.

610
00:27:47.820 --> 00:27:48.420
All right.

611
00:27:48.420 --> 00:27:52.160
I also talked to Randall about this, and he said this was totally on purpose.

612
00:27:52.520 --> 00:28:00.240
He planted a guy standing at reception in the same way that Jim would always stand at reception and talk to Pam.

613
00:28:01.000 --> 00:28:06.520
He even has his sleeves rolled up the way Jim used to roll up his sleeves.

614
00:28:06.940 --> 00:28:07.500
Sleeve gate.

615
00:28:07.720 --> 00:28:09.720
Susan, are you listening to this, Susan?

616
00:28:09.940 --> 00:28:10.180
Yeah.

617
00:28:10.500 --> 00:28:10.940
I know.

618
00:28:11.080 --> 00:28:12.360
Susan who broke down the sleeves.

619
00:28:12.980 --> 00:28:13.480
Yeah.

620
00:28:13.480 --> 00:28:20.720
And Randall said that was on purpose to keep that Pam-Jim thing sort of in our orbit.

621
00:28:21.080 --> 00:28:22.020
Oh, my gosh.

622
00:28:22.060 --> 00:28:23.280
That's such a beautiful detail.

623
00:28:23.480 --> 00:28:28.160
So Stanford has their own version of Jim and Pam that hang out at reception and flirt.

624
00:28:28.760 --> 00:28:28.920
Yeah.

625
00:28:30.180 --> 00:28:31.040
Amazing, right?

626
00:28:31.220 --> 00:28:31.940
That's amazing.

627
00:28:32.640 --> 00:28:33.260
All right.

628
00:28:33.260 --> 00:28:35.540
So let's go back to the Scranton branch.

629
00:28:36.080 --> 00:28:38.360
Pam is keeping notes on Michael's day.

630
00:28:38.600 --> 00:28:42.460
He's on a phone call, and he's doing a Bill Cosby impression.

631
00:28:43.400 --> 00:28:49.060
So Pam holds up her log, and she has written Cosby impression.

632
00:28:49.360 --> 00:28:53.260
So first of all, that is not my handwriting on the log, people asked.

633
00:28:53.460 --> 00:28:56.120
And also, we got a fan question from Jonathan Barak.

634
00:28:56.280 --> 00:29:00.500
When Jan gives Pam a timesheet to write down everything Michael does throughout the day,

635
00:29:00.640 --> 00:29:02.420
why doesn't Pam cover for him?

636
00:29:03.220 --> 00:29:07.400
Like, why not write down sales call instead of Cosby impression?

637
00:29:07.780 --> 00:29:08.080
Hmm.

638
00:29:08.480 --> 00:29:09.640
What are our thoughts on that?

639
00:29:10.020 --> 00:29:13.120
I mean, Pam sometimes is a little bit of a stickler.

640
00:29:13.600 --> 00:29:15.580
She's a rule follower, you know?

641
00:29:16.620 --> 00:29:17.020
Yeah.

642
00:29:17.120 --> 00:29:22.040
I mean, I think if she covers for Michael and then Jan finds out otherwise,

643
00:29:22.780 --> 00:29:23.940
Pam's going to get in trouble.

644
00:29:24.920 --> 00:29:25.400
Yeah.

645
00:29:25.520 --> 00:29:28.420
I mean, I feel like she does cover for him later.

646
00:29:28.420 --> 00:29:31.880
So maybe she starts out being like, all right, I'll do what Jan told me.

647
00:29:32.420 --> 00:29:36.640
But then she feels bad or something because Michael is such a train wreck for the whole day.

648
00:29:37.360 --> 00:29:37.520
Yeah.

649
00:29:37.880 --> 00:29:40.620
And he ends up making a huge sale in the end.

650
00:29:40.980 --> 00:29:43.980
So that's the thing about Michael, right?

651
00:29:44.220 --> 00:29:48.680
Like, his weird ways, they turn out.

652
00:29:48.680 --> 00:29:50.480
He's able to connect with people.

653
00:29:50.720 --> 00:29:51.160
He is.

654
00:29:51.340 --> 00:29:52.740
Say what you want to say about Michael.

655
00:29:53.280 --> 00:29:54.260
People like him.

656
00:29:54.380 --> 00:29:56.140
Not that have to work with him.

657
00:29:56.640 --> 00:29:56.760
Yeah.

658
00:29:57.140 --> 00:29:58.500
Acquaintances really like him.

659
00:29:58.860 --> 00:29:59.060
All right.

660
00:29:59.120 --> 00:30:00.760
Now we're back at the Stanford branch.

661
00:30:01.000 --> 00:30:06.300
And Andy, I guess, has told Karen he'll try to help her, you know, get that chair back.

662
00:30:06.300 --> 00:30:09.300
So he's like, Jim, can you come talk to me over here?

663
00:30:09.460 --> 00:30:11.200
And he's like, I can talk to you from here.

664
00:30:11.280 --> 00:30:13.280
And he's like, yeah, but could you get up and come over here?

665
00:30:13.440 --> 00:30:14.740
And Karen's like, that's all you got?

666
00:30:15.200 --> 00:30:21.180
And then Ed as Andy made me laugh so hard when he says, I'm acting my heart out here.

667
00:30:21.920 --> 00:30:26.940
I mean, Andy is so bad.

668
00:30:26.980 --> 00:30:28.180
He's so transparent.

669
00:30:28.180 --> 00:30:31.420
And it just was so funny that he was like, hey, come on.

670
00:30:32.180 --> 00:30:37.700
That seemed like an Ed improv to me, but I couldn't check because this storyline isn't in my script.

671
00:30:38.120 --> 00:30:40.840
We did have a fan catch at this moment.

672
00:30:41.440 --> 00:30:47.820
Marguerite Carter wrote in to say, at around 4 minutes, 50 seconds, you can see Jim's nameplate on his desk.

673
00:30:47.840 --> 00:30:49.700
And it says, Jim Halpert.

674
00:30:50.040 --> 00:30:53.080
And underneath it, it says, Assistant Regional Manager.

675
00:30:53.980 --> 00:30:54.540
Marguerite.

676
00:30:54.880 --> 00:30:55.800
There you go.

677
00:30:56.240 --> 00:30:56.920
Applause.

678
00:30:57.680 --> 00:30:58.220
It's official.

679
00:30:58.760 --> 00:31:03.880
Well, things are about to get real exciting over at the Scranton branch because it is announced.

680
00:31:03.880 --> 00:31:04.960
It's Pretzel Day.

681
00:31:05.620 --> 00:31:08.040
Oh, and Stanley books it out the door.

682
00:31:08.200 --> 00:31:10.240
He books it, but he grabs his crossword.

683
00:31:11.540 --> 00:31:12.900
Well, he knows.

684
00:31:13.080 --> 00:31:14.580
He knows there's going to be a line.

685
00:31:14.960 --> 00:31:15.060
Yeah.

686
00:31:15.180 --> 00:31:15.320
Yeah.

687
00:31:15.480 --> 00:31:16.560
He's planning ahead.

688
00:31:16.880 --> 00:31:18.000
He's ready for it.

689
00:31:18.140 --> 00:31:20.680
I have a question, and this was also a fan catch.

690
00:31:20.880 --> 00:31:25.080
Kelly Rue asked, whose voice makes the announcement about Pretzel Day?

691
00:31:25.080 --> 00:31:29.040
And Bailey Aspenson said, is that the voice of Billy Merchant?

692
00:31:29.280 --> 00:31:29.760
It is.

693
00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:31.020
It's Billy Merchant.

694
00:31:31.200 --> 00:31:31.700
Yes.

695
00:31:31.980 --> 00:31:34.220
Which makes sense because he's the building manager.

696
00:31:34.620 --> 00:31:34.920
Yeah.

697
00:31:35.120 --> 00:31:39.380
I asked Kent Sabornak because there was a part of me that thought, is that Kent's voice?

698
00:31:39.620 --> 00:31:41.000
And Kent said, no.

699
00:31:41.480 --> 00:31:46.180
Kent said they got Marcus York to record that audio for the announcement.

700
00:31:47.020 --> 00:31:47.560
Amazing.

701
00:31:47.800 --> 00:31:48.220
I love it.

702
00:31:48.220 --> 00:31:49.060
I love the attention to detail.

703
00:31:49.060 --> 00:31:50.040
I love the attention to detail.

704
00:31:50.420 --> 00:31:51.380
I do, too.

705
00:31:51.780 --> 00:31:57.260
So now we have the Stanley talking head that is one of my favorite talking heads, and Leslie's

706
00:31:57.260 --> 00:31:58.760
delivery of it is so great.

707
00:31:58.980 --> 00:32:04.520
He says, I wake up every morning in a bed that's too small, drive my daughter to school

708
00:32:04.520 --> 00:32:08.840
that's too expensive, and then I go to work for a job, which I get paid too little.

709
00:32:09.380 --> 00:32:10.720
But on Pretzel Day?

710
00:32:11.520 --> 00:32:13.420
Well, I like Pretzel Day.

711
00:32:14.920 --> 00:32:20.060
That talking head that appears in the episode is an alternate talking head, Angela, that

712
00:32:20.060 --> 00:32:22.060
is not the talking head that was in the shooting draft.

713
00:32:22.140 --> 00:32:23.360
It's so perfect, though.

714
00:32:23.420 --> 00:32:24.940
I can't imagine anything else.

715
00:32:25.120 --> 00:32:26.500
I mean, it's so perfect.

716
00:32:26.680 --> 00:32:28.560
It sums up, like, Stanley.

717
00:32:29.360 --> 00:32:30.260
What was it?

718
00:32:30.480 --> 00:32:32.700
The original talking head was this.

719
00:32:33.040 --> 00:32:35.020
There are four things that I love.

720
00:32:35.020 --> 00:32:40.820
My wife, my daughter, Pam Greer, and a hot, chewy roll of buttered dough.

721
00:32:41.240 --> 00:32:42.280
Pam Greer.

722
00:32:43.520 --> 00:32:44.100
All right.

723
00:32:44.300 --> 00:32:46.040
Fan question from Crystal White.

724
00:32:46.400 --> 00:32:52.500
Who came up with the idea of Pretzel Day, and were there actually pretzels for the cast

725
00:32:52.500 --> 00:32:53.740
to eat on set?

726
00:32:54.340 --> 00:32:56.560
Thank you so much for your question, Crystal.

727
00:32:56.860 --> 00:32:59.620
Okay, so we got a lot of pretzel-related questions.

728
00:33:00.660 --> 00:33:04.900
Jenna, I know you did some digging around, and you got the real deal on this.

729
00:33:05.020 --> 00:33:07.120
Yes, I found out some good stuff.

730
00:33:07.300 --> 00:33:13.400
First off, BJ told me that the inspiration for Pretzel Day goes all the way back to season

731
00:33:13.400 --> 00:33:13.800
one.

732
00:33:14.480 --> 00:33:20.120
Greg took the writers on these fact-finding visits to real offices so that they could

733
00:33:20.120 --> 00:33:22.760
get inspiration for storylines and episodes.

734
00:33:23.120 --> 00:33:24.040
I love that.

735
00:33:24.160 --> 00:33:26.720
Now, I know that Greg likes to do stuff like this.

736
00:33:26.800 --> 00:33:31.520
I know when he was working on King of the Hill, he took all the writers to Austin, Texas,

737
00:33:31.720 --> 00:33:34.760
because they were like, okay, you got to know Texas if you're writing about

738
00:33:34.760 --> 00:33:35.820
characters that live in Texas.

739
00:33:35.820 --> 00:33:40.100
So I love that Greg is now taking our writers to small businesses.

740
00:33:40.780 --> 00:33:43.740
Yes, and Lady, he even took them to a paper company.

741
00:33:43.960 --> 00:33:44.840
Oh, my God.

742
00:33:45.120 --> 00:33:45.560
Amazing.

743
00:33:46.080 --> 00:33:46.480
Yes.

744
00:33:46.680 --> 00:33:51.660
And BJ told me that on one of the days when they went to this office park, they were doing

745
00:33:51.660 --> 00:33:55.800
some kind of morale-boosting free food giveaway for the employees.

746
00:33:55.820 --> 00:33:56.880
It wasn't pretzels.

747
00:33:56.880 --> 00:34:02.740
He thinks maybe it was popcorn, but everyone was really excited, and the writers put this

748
00:34:02.740 --> 00:34:09.320
idea on a card in the writer's room, and then BJ added the tidbit that Stanley would

749
00:34:09.320 --> 00:34:12.940
be the character that looked forward to this free food giveaway all year.

750
00:34:13.140 --> 00:34:14.320
I love that.

751
00:34:14.440 --> 00:34:16.840
I love that so much, and it's so relatable.

752
00:34:17.280 --> 00:34:23.960
